WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.029
Dijkstra's algorithm finds the cheapest path when every edge has a weight.

2
00:00:05.329 --> 00:00:10.811
It repeats two moves: settle the closest vertex, then relax the edges that leave it.

3
00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:15.080
Vertex C is settled at four, and vertex B still has six.

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.686
The edge from C to B weighs one, so the route through C costs four plus one, which

5
00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:21.204
is five.

6
00:00:21.504 --> 00:00:26.987
Five beats six, so relaxation lowers B to five and records C as its parent.

7
00:00:27.437 --> 00:00:29.421
Start at S with distance zero.

8
00:00:29.721 --> 00:00:34.990
Each time, settle the smallest open value: S, then E at one, then A at two.

9
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:40.154
Settling C at four lowers B from six to five and opens G at nine.

10
00:00:40.454 --> 00:00:45.083
B gives D seven, and D lowers G to eight, the cheapest cost to G.

11
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:48.392
This only works when no edge weight is negative.

12
00:00:48.692 --> 00:00:51.892
Here X is settled at two before Y is looked at.

13
00:00:52.192 --> 00:00:57.525
But Y costs three, and its edge to X weighs minus two, so X really costs one.

14
00:00:57.825 --> 00:01:02.263
A settled value is final, so Dijkstra keeps the wrong distance of two.

15
00:01:02.713 --> 00:01:08.366
So: settle the smallest tentative distance, relax its edges, and keep every weight

16
00:01:08.366 --> 00:01:09.262
non-negative.
